<details>
<summary><b>Steps of Reproduction ✅ </b></summary>

```mdx
1. Locate the filesystem tools definition in
`lib/mastra/tools/filesystem-tools.ts:46-207`; note the `search_files` tool whose
`execute` implementation at lines 173-206 calls `virtualFilesystem.getWorkspace(userId)`.

2. Observe the virtual filesystem implementation in
`lib/virtual-filesystem/virtual-filesystem-service.ts:31-642`; this defines methods like
`readFile`, `writeFile`, `listDirectory`, `search`, `getWorkspaceVersion`, and
`exportWorkspace` (lines 355-367), but there is no `getWorkspace` method anywhere in the
class.

3. In any runtime context (e.g., a test or script), import the tools array and invoke the
search tool directly:

   - `import { filesystemTools } from '@/lib/mastra/tools/filesystem-tools';`

   - `const tool = filesystemTools.find(t => t.name === 'search_files')!;`

   - `await tool.execute({ query: 'todo', path: 'project' });`

   When `execute` runs, it reaches line 176 (`const snapshot = await
   virtualFilesystem.getWorkspace(userId);`).

4. At this point Node attempts to call a non-existent method on the `virtualFilesystem`
singleton exported from `lib/virtual-filesystem/virtual-filesystem-service.ts:644`, and
throws `TypeError: virtualFilesystem.getWorkspace is not a function`, causing the
`search_files` tool (and any higher-level `AgentLoop` caller at
`lib/mastra/agent-loop.ts:74-165` that executes this tool) to fail the whole iteration.
```
