The WebSocket terminal endpoint allows connections without authentication and trusts a client-supplied `token` query parameter as the `userId`. This lets any unauthenticated user connect to arbitrary `sessionId`/`sandboxId` pairs.

```ts
if (pathname === '/api/sandbox/terminal/ws') {
  const sessionId = query.sessionId as string;
  const sandboxId = query.sandboxId as string;
  const token = query.token as string;

  if (!sessionId || !sandboxId) {
    socket.destroy();
    return;
  }

  // Authenticate the connection
  // In production, validate the token against your auth system
  const userId = token || 'anonymous';

  wss.handleUpgrade(req, socket, head, (ws) => {
    wss.emit('connection', ws, req, { sessionId, sandboxId, userId });
  });
}
```

Impact: Unauthenticated users can establish WebSocket sessions and potentially interact with terminal streams once integrated, enabling unauthorized access or manipulation. Passing identity in query parameters also risks token leakage via logs/referrers and makes spoofing trivial.

Remediation:
- Enforce authentication during the upgrade: verify a JWT or session using your existing auth (e.g., `verifyAuth` from `lib/auth/jwt.ts`) and reject if invalid.
- Do not accept identity from query params; derive `userId` from the verified token/session.
- Verify that the authenticated `userId` owns the specified `sandboxId`/`sessionId` before completing the upgrade.
- Optionally validate the `Origin` header and rate-limit connections to mitigate abuse.

For more details, see the [finding in Corridor](https://app.corridor.dev/projects/9b474c20-ed45-4b3a-ad03-b76d21c45b95/findings/f202c543-0e77-44ef-b081-6b8fc929d08d).

