The WebSocket upgrade handler does not validate the request Origin. Without an Origin allowlist, a malicious site can initiate a cross-site WebSocket request to this endpoint (e.g., if session cookies or ambient credentials are present), enabling cross-site WebSocket hijacking attempts and unintended terminal connections.

Vulnerable area:
```ts
server.on('upgrade', (req: IncomingMessage, socket, head) => {
  const { pathname, query } = parse(req.url || '', true);
  // ... no Origin validation before handleUpgrade
  if (pathname === '/api/sandbox/terminal/ws') {
    // ...
    wss.handleUpgrade(req, socket, head, (ws) => {
      wss.emit('connection', ws, req, { sessionId, sandboxId, userId });
    });
  }
});
```
Remediation:
- Check the `Origin` header against an allowlist (e.g., your app's origin) before calling `handleUpgrade`, and reject mismatches.
- Consider requiring `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` with a bearer token to prevent unauthorized cross-origin connections.

For more details, see the [finding in Corridor](https://app.corridor.dev/projects/9b474c20-ed45-4b3a-ad03-b76d21c45b95/findings/99a92cbe-ab6f-4d8c-a19a-022d440f564b).

